<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/wildfire.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/wildfire-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/wildfire.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="682" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="shell sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="/utah-appraisal-services/">Services</a><span class="sep">›</span><span aria-current="page">Wildfire Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Wildfire Insurance Claim Appraisal in Utah</h1><p class="lede">More Utah homes sit where neighborhoods meet brush, oak and juniper. When a wildfire comes through, claims range from total losses to smoke and ash that are easy to underprice. We value both.</p><div class="row-btns"><a class="btn btn-accent" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/contact-utah-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Risk areas</dt><dd>Foothills &amp; canyons</dd></div><div><dt>Notable fires</dt><dd>Brian Head, Pole Creek</dd></div><div><dt>Season</dt><dd>June–October</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="shell two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Utah’s wildland-urban interface</h2>
<p>Utah’s growth has pushed homes into the foothills of the Wasatch and Oquirrh mountains, the canyons of Summit and Wasatch counties and the juniper country of southern Utah. The 2017 Brian Head Fire burned homes and cabins in Iron and Garfield counties. In 2018, the Pole Creek and Bald Mountain fires forced large evacuations in south Utah County. Each fire season brings new starts.</p>
<h2>Two very different wildfire claims</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Total and major losses.</strong> These involve policy limits, extended replacement cost, debris removal, code upgrades and additional living expenses. The challenge is making sure the rebuild estimate reflects today’s construction costs.</li>
<li><strong>Smoke and ash.</strong> Homes that didn’t burn can still have smoke in HVAC systems, insulation and soft goods, plus ash on roofs and in landscaping. These claims are frequently underpaid because the damage isn’t obvious.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What we document</h2>
<p>Ash and char deposits, soot on surfaces, filtration and HVAC contamination, heat damage to windows, siding and vinyl, and damage to fences, sheds, decks and landscaping. Testing by an environmental professional can support cleaning and replacement scope.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Underinsurance</b>After a wildfire, the biggest gap is often a policy limit that’s too low, not an estimate. Appraisal can’t raise a limit. It can make sure every covered dollar up to that limit is accounted for.</div>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My house didn’t burn but smells like smoke. Is that a claim?<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="ans"><p>It may be. Smoke infiltration can be covered physical damage under many policies. Document it early and consider professional testing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is landscaping covered after a wildfire?<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="ans"><p>Many policies cover trees, shrubs and plants with a limit per plant and an overall cap. Check the ‘trees, shrubs and other plants’ provision.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long do I have to rebuild?<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="ans"><p>Policies set time limits to recover replacement cost. Some states extend them after declared disasters. Confirm the deadline for your policy in writing.</p></div></details></div><h2>Related services</h2><div class="rel-grid"><a class="rel" href="/utah-appraisal-services/earthquake-claim-appraisal/"><small>Service</small><span>Earthquake Claim Appraisal</span><svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/utah-appraisal-services/insurance-estimate-audit/"><small>Service</small><span>Insurance Estimate Review</span><svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a><a class="rel" href="/utah-appraisal-services/appraisal-umpire-services/"><small>Service</small><span>Umpire Services</span><svg class="ico sm" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"